# ShinboShark local rule layer - corrections to the vendored daffainfo set.
#
# Origin of the corrected rule: backend/rules/daffainfo/http/web-attacks/
# path-traversal.rules, SID 50500002, vendored verbatim from
# daffainfo/suricata-rules @23bfba7e688fdf5fadd8e378f070dae843e14263.
#
# Vendored text:
#   alert http any any -> any any (msg:"Possible Path Traversal attack, repeated
#     dot-dot-backslash sequence"; content:"..|5C|"; http_uri;
#     pcre:"/(?:\\.\\){2,}/U"; classtype:web-application-attack;
#     sid:50500002; rev:1;)
#
# DEFECT (demonstrated, see backend/rules/RULE-AUDIT.md): the rule could not
# alert on its own target - "GET /..\..\..\windows\win.ini" produced no
# SID 50500002 finding - for three independent reasons:
#
#   1. The pcre mode "U" (URL-decode the inspected buffer) is not implemented by
#      this matcher. A rule carrying it is stored with supported = 0 and reported
#      as skipped, so this rule was never evaluated at all.
#   2. The pattern never compiled. Taking the pattern out of its quoted option
#      collapses the two-character sequence backslash-backslash into a single
#      backslash, which here removed the escape guarding the group's closing
#      bracket: the bytes handed to the regex engine became hex
#      28 3f 3a 5c 2e 5c 2e 5c 29 7b 32 2c 7d, i.e. the group's ")" is escaped and
#      never closed. Python's re rejected it with "missing ), unterminated
#      subpattern at position 0". A pcre that does not compile raised no error
#      and matched nothing - a silent false negative.
#   3. The buffer this matcher holds carries TShark's escaped field text:
#      TShark writes one backslash on the wire as two in "-T fields" output (the
#      runner passes -E separator/occurrence but not -E escape), so the indexed
#      request target for a single-backslash URI is "..\\..\\", not "..\..\".
#      A pattern requiring exactly one backslash between the ".." pairs can
#      therefore never match an indexed URI.
#
# CORRECTION, and why each part is safe:
#   * The backslash is written as the hex escape \x5c, which is identical in
#     PCRE and in Python's engine and survives the unquoting step. (Reason 2.)
#   * The separator accepts one OR two backslashes. The one-backslash form is
#     the wire form and is what Suricata itself inspects, so nothing is lost
#     there; the two-backslash form is how this matcher's indexed buffer spells
#     the same bytes, so the rule finally matches its own target here. (Reason 3.)
#   * The unimplementable U mode is dropped. (Reason 1.) The rule's own
#     "content:\"..|5C|\"; http_uri" already required a literal backslash in
#     the request target, so the U mode could only have helped a mixed
#     literal/percent-encoded form - and that form is covered by the sibling
#     rules 50500004/50500005, which match "..%2f" and "..%5c" directly.
#
# Detection objective, SID and classtype are unchanged; "rev" was raised to 2
# because the detection logic changed.
#
# Verified: GET /..\..\..\windows\win.ini now alerts (it did not before);
# GET /a..\b/c.txt, which has a single "..\" and is not a repeated traversal,
# stays silent, as does GET /a/../b.txt.
#
# NOTE: the vendored file is third-party data and is deliberately left
# unmodified - see README.md, "Adding your own rules".

alert http any any -> any any (msg:"Possible Path Traversal attack, repeated dot-dot-backslash sequence"; content:"..|5C|"; http_uri; pcre:"/(?:\.\.\x5c{1,2}){2,}/"; classtype:web-application-attack; sid:50500002; rev:2;)
